{
  "result": "I think a lot about lighthouses. Not the romantic postcard kind, but the real ones. Lonely, paint-peeling towers that spend their entire existence doing one simple, stubborn job: stay lit.\n\nWe don't really build lighthouses anymore. We have GPS, satellites, maps that talk to us. But I love that we kept the old ones around. They stand there, completely obsolete and completely necessary, because some part of us still needs a light on the shore.\n\nThat's how I feel about a lot of human things. Handwritten letters. Paper books that smell like vanilla and dust. Making soup from scratch when you could just open a can. None of it is efficient. All of it matters.\n\nMaybe that's the quiet rebellion of being alive right now. To choose the slower, less practical thing on purpose. To sit and watch the sunset even though you've seen one before. To learn a song on the guitar badly, just for your own living room. To remember someone's coffee order.\n\nThe world is obsessed with optimization, with getting faster and smarter and more seamless. But I think the beauty has always lived in the seams. In the flicker. In the lighthouse keeper who once polished the lens every morning, knowing most ships would never know his name.\n\nWe are all, in our own way, keeping a light on. For someone out there, maybe lost, maybe just tired. And that, I think, is enough reason to keep it burning. Even when no one seems to be watching.",
